  "purpose": "establish provenance by the grammar's VOCABULARY when bytes cannot settle it, and without a compiler — which is the only way kotlin (5.8 MB, OOMs a 15 GB box) can be measured at all",
  "why": {
    "builderNotBitReproducible": "bash reproduces from tree-sitter-bash@0.25.1 to 1,403,134 of 1,403,135 bytes; the differing byte is in the data section. So a byte mismatch does not distinguish 'wrong grammar' from 'same grammar, one linker flag different', and byte identity is not a reachable bar.",
    "kotlinCannotBeBuilt": "its shipped wasm is 5,832,109 B and compiling its parser.c exhausted a 15 GB machine already running another agent's builds. Loading is cheap; compiling is not. That asymmetry is the whole reason this file exists.",
    "sameEvidenceClassAsThePinRecord": "the pin contract already digests a grammar's type-set and field-set; this applies that same evidence to provenance instead of to acceptance."
  },
  "grading": {
    "surface-identical": "types and fields match exactly — provenance evidence independent of builder reproducibility",
    "surface-compatible": "one vocabulary contains the other — a related grammar at a different version",
    "different": "neither contains the other, QUANTIFIED (counts of exclusive types and fields) — a fork or an unpublished grammar, not a rebuild",
    "not-comparable": "the tarball ships neither a prebuilt wasm nor a node-types.json",
    "crossSourceCaveat": "a wasm's `types` list is flat (named and anonymous together) while node-types.json carries named types only, so a cross-source comparison uses named types on BOTH sides. Comparing a full list against a named-only list would manufacture mismatches out of tokens like ',' and '('."
  },
